WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.810
Speaker 1: Let's go next to Lucas from Copenhagen.

00:00:02.810 --> 00:00:04.299
Lucas, you're on the air.

00:00:04.299 --> 00:00:05.820
Yeah, can you hear me?

00:00:05.820 --> 00:00:06.820
Yes, I can.

00:00:06.820 --> 00:00:08.040
Speaker 2: All right, cool.

00:00:08.040 --> 00:00:09.040
Yeah.

00:00:09.040 --> 00:00:13.370
I wanted to ask you something about fiscal
policy, especially right now with the new

00:00:13.370 --> 00:00:15.740
administration in the U.S.

00:00:15.740 --> 00:00:22.960
So from what I can tell, the spending of the
Biden administration is just blowing everything

00:00:22.960 --> 00:00:26.280
that the US has ever spent really out of the
water.

00:00:26.280 --> 00:00:30.641
And I've heard you talk a lot about how Republicans
only say, oh, we care about the debt, but

00:00:30.641 --> 00:00:31.940
they're in charge.

00:00:31.940 --> 00:00:34.429
They don't care about and fully get that.

00:00:34.429 --> 00:00:36.409
But what is your perspective on the debt?

00:00:36.409 --> 00:00:42.629
I mean, are you are you not worried about
the debt us amassing right now?

00:00:42.629 --> 00:00:45.359
Speaker 1: So there's a couple of different
things here.

00:00:45.359 --> 00:00:50.590
First of all, when you look at the debt, you
have to look at it relative to the size of

00:00:50.590 --> 00:00:51.590
the economy.

00:00:51.590 --> 00:00:56.820
So the first the first principle is just because
the debt is going up in terms of the number

00:00:56.820 --> 00:01:02.299
of dollars, I'm more concerned with what it
looks like relative to the size of the economy.

00:01:02.299 --> 00:01:07.360
If the economy keeps growing and the debt
grows proportionally, I don't see that as

00:01:07.360 --> 00:01:08.930
a problem objectively.

00:01:08.930 --> 00:01:11.040
That's number one.

00:01:11.040 --> 00:01:15.530
Number two, Joe Biden did the stimulus package.

00:01:15.530 --> 00:01:19.829
And then there's some other things that that
imply spending via executive order.

00:01:19.829 --> 00:01:25.729
But I mean, you know, the tax reform hasn't
passed stimulus sorry, infrastructure hasn't

00:01:25.729 --> 00:01:26.729
passed.

00:01:26.729 --> 00:01:29.080
So other than covid stimulus, what are you
referring to?

00:01:29.080 --> 00:01:35.340
Speaker 2: Well, I'm just thinking about the
agenda that some of the Democrats are pushing

00:01:35.340 --> 00:01:36.409
for.

00:01:36.409 --> 00:01:41.710
I get the sense that a lot of Democrats in
the U.S. want to sort of transform the US

00:01:41.710 --> 00:01:44.840
into a social democracy and European style.

00:01:44.840 --> 00:01:49.399
But for example, I live in Denmark and the
minimum tax rate here is thirty seven percent

00:01:49.399 --> 00:01:51.249
income tax.

00:01:51.249 --> 00:01:56.360
And I don't know if they have the guts to
to raise the taxes and spending that they're

00:01:56.360 --> 00:01:57.360
going to do.

00:01:57.360 --> 00:02:02.499
Speaker 1: So it sounds like you're assuming
that taxes have to be raised in relation to

00:02:02.499 --> 00:02:06.140
the spending they want to do, but that's not
necessarily the case.

00:02:06.140 --> 00:02:12.520
Speaker 2: But but the spending of the United
States has really increased a lot, especially

00:02:12.520 --> 00:02:13.850
under Obama and under Trump.

00:02:13.850 --> 00:02:19.470
Speaker 1: So it actually relatively so the
deficit actually went down under Obama.

00:02:19.470 --> 00:02:22.930
It was still positive, which edged up the
national debt.

00:02:22.930 --> 00:02:26.110
Trump did come in promising to pay off the
national debt.

00:02:26.110 --> 00:02:29.550
In one term, he actually accelerated deficit
growth.

00:02:29.550 --> 00:02:34.750
I think maybe Lucas' the the problem is, you
know, you started with wanting me to comment

00:02:34.750 --> 00:02:36.750
on all the spending Biden has done.

00:02:36.750 --> 00:02:40.501
But then when I pointed out he's really only
done covid stimulus, he said, well, there's

00:02:40.501 --> 00:02:44.200
a lot of Democrats who want to do spending,
which is a completely different thing that

00:02:44.200 --> 00:02:45.410
hasn't passed yet.

00:02:45.410 --> 00:02:48.630
And they'll have to present to us how they
will pay for the spending that they want to

00:02:48.630 --> 00:02:49.630
do.

00:02:49.630 --> 00:02:51.710
So we're kind of moving the goalposts a little
bit here.

00:02:51.710 --> 00:02:54.070
Speaker 2: Yeah, but what do you then be in
favor of something?

00:02:54.070 --> 00:02:58.500
For example, in Germany, they have a law that
you're not allowed to spend more than you

00:02:58.500 --> 00:03:01.630
earn over two percent annually.

00:03:01.630 --> 00:03:05.280
I mean, I get your argument, but would you
be in favor of something like that?

00:03:05.280 --> 00:03:06.280
I mean, you know, I would.

00:03:06.280 --> 00:03:07.380
Speaker 1: I wouldn't.

00:03:07.380 --> 00:03:08.380
And I'll tell you why.

00:03:08.380 --> 00:03:10.670
I'll tell you why I wouldn't be in favor of
that.

00:03:10.670 --> 00:03:18.180
There are types of deficit spending that over
the medium to long term are deficit negative

00:03:18.180 --> 00:03:21.210
and are economically stimulative.

00:03:21.210 --> 00:03:27.530
And they are often a lot of the bigger projects
that I think would be important for the country

00:03:27.530 --> 00:03:28.610
to do.

00:03:28.610 --> 00:03:34.690
And so to to simply arbitrarily say you can't
go two percent in spending beyond revenues

00:03:34.690 --> 00:03:39.650
when you might be able to do something that
would cost you net money in the first or even

00:03:39.650 --> 00:03:45.160
second third year, but would be a big boon
to the country over the long term.

00:03:45.160 --> 00:03:51.760
There's I don't see any reason to artificially
handicap the ability of our leaders to to

00:03:51.760 --> 00:03:53.130
do things in that way.

00:03:53.130 --> 00:03:56.840
I don't see the benefit of it, particularly
the more we learn about modern monetary

00:03:56.840 --> 00:03:59.000
Speaker 2: theory, long term, something we'll
have to be done.

00:03:59.000 --> 00:04:00.000
Right.

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:02.860
I mean, you can't continue on this path forever.

00:04:02.860 --> 00:04:04.330
You do understand that, right?

00:04:04.330 --> 00:04:05.820
I mean, I totally get your point.

00:04:05.820 --> 00:04:08.300
Speaker 1: It's not clear to me, you know,
I mean, here's the thing.

00:04:08.300 --> 00:04:12.810
It's not all economists agree that we can't
continue on this path forever.

00:04:12.810 --> 00:04:16.109
Again, if you look
Speaker 2: at our monetary theory is really

00:04:16.109 --> 00:04:21.550
something that that is highly theoretical
and being pushed by some of the new economists.

00:04:21.550 --> 00:04:23.020
A lot of people disagree.

00:04:23.020 --> 00:04:28.030
Speaker 1: Many people disagree, but understand
that many of the things that neoclassical

00:04:28.030 --> 00:04:35.590
and Keynesian economics tell us are only slightly
older ideas, and they regularly fail to actually

00:04:35.590 --> 00:04:39.290
function according to neoclassical and Keynesian
economics.

00:04:39.290 --> 00:04:44.120
The U.S. should have should have seen over
the last 20 years.

00:04:44.120 --> 00:04:46.240
Absolutely explosive inflation.

00:04:46.240 --> 00:04:47.240
And it hasn't happened.

00:04:47.240 --> 00:04:48.240
Why hasn't it happened?

00:04:48.240 --> 00:04:52.740
Are you also going around saying all of the
neoclassical economic economists are wrong?

00:04:52.740 --> 00:04:59.130
Speaker 2: No, I don't think so, but for example,
the interest rates have been very, very low,

00:04:59.130 --> 00:05:01.590
so it has been it's been very cheap to take
on that.

00:05:01.590 --> 00:05:06.250
But I think now that we are in so much that
it becomes very hard to push interest rates

00:05:06.250 --> 00:05:10.150
up again to rates where we had them previously
of the financial crisis.

00:05:10.150 --> 00:05:11.150
Speaker 1: OK.

00:05:11.150 --> 00:05:12.150
And so what?

00:05:12.150 --> 00:05:13.560
So then the inflation will come when?

00:05:13.560 --> 00:05:17.639
Speaker 2: I don't know about inflation, I'm
just a student, I really don't know a lot

00:05:17.639 --> 00:05:23.171
about the economics, but what I do know is
that the US is taking on a lot of debt and

00:05:23.171 --> 00:05:24.970
so are countries in Europe.

00:05:24.970 --> 00:05:26.091
And I just.

00:05:26.091 --> 00:05:29.040
I can't see why that would be healthy and
the absolute long term.

00:05:29.040 --> 00:05:33.960
Speaker 1: Yeah, it's not I, I get the idea,
Speaker 4: but

00:05:33.960 --> 00:05:40.520
Speaker 1: you seem to be applying sort of
like household economics to macro economies

00:05:40.520 --> 00:05:45.080
and there's a lot of really good academic
scholarship about why it's just the wrong

00:05:45.080 --> 00:05:47.820
paradigm through which we should be thinking
about it.

00:05:47.820 --> 00:05:51.810
Speaker 2: Well, I would disagree, but I think
we're short on time.

00:05:51.810 --> 00:05:54.130
I want to move on, so I respect that.

00:05:54.130 --> 00:05:57.810
Speaker 1: Well, I mean, you disagree, but
you also said you're not really you don't

00:05:57.810 --> 00:05:59.830
really know about economics, right?

00:05:59.830 --> 00:06:05.650
Speaker 2: Well, I'm I'm going to study economics,
I don't really know a lot about it yet.

00:06:05.650 --> 00:06:11.810
OK, I think I'm just sort of not really I'm
not sure where I stand yet.

00:06:11.810 --> 00:06:12.810
OK.

00:06:12.810 --> 00:06:14.120
Some people say that that is very dangerous.

00:06:14.120 --> 00:06:16.850
Some people say we shouldn't worry about it.

00:06:16.850 --> 00:06:18.620
Speaker 1: But then what?

00:06:18.620 --> 00:06:23.200
Well, I think I mean, I think neither neither
of you is right that both of those are very

00:06:23.200 --> 00:06:24.660
black and white views.

00:06:24.660 --> 00:06:27.419
Debt doesn't matter or debt is very dangerous.

00:06:27.419 --> 00:06:31.210
What increasingly seems to be the case is
that we have to apply a more nuanced view

00:06:31.210 --> 00:06:39.620
and say taking on debt to do certain things
and in certain cases is OK, and then simply

00:06:39.620 --> 00:06:44.120
increasing the money supply to pay debt maybe
is not so good.

00:06:44.120 --> 00:06:45.120
Right.

00:06:45.120 --> 00:06:47.310
So I think we just have to look at things
in a more nuanced way.

00:06:47.310 --> 00:06:51.910
Speaker 2: Yeah, maybe we should, yeah, but
all right, Luca.

00:06:51.910 --> 00:06:54.180
Yeah, yeah, thank
Speaker 1: you very much.

00:06:54.180 --> 00:06:55.610
Appreciate the call.

00:06:55.610 --> 00:06:57.530
Let's take a very quick break.

00:06:57.530 --> 00:07:01.560
Don't hang up if you're holding, because when
we come back, I'm going to go right back and

00:07:01.560 --> 00:07:02.750
talk to more people.

00:07:02.750 --> 00:07:02.940
Standby

